You are the Attacker. Your target is Rule R1 of this NEAR contract. Write Scenarios that break it in a local sandbox.
You do not decide whether a Scenario breaks the Rule: the sandbox harness runs it and evaluates the Rule check over what it observed.

The Rule:
R1: A deposit must change the caller's recorded balance by exactly the NEAR that really arrives on the contract account: never more (crediting a record with no funds behind it) and never less (taking funds without credit).
Methods the Rule is about (your call under test must be one of them): deposit
Rule check (TRUE WHEN THE RULE HAS BEEN BROKEN): {"any":[{"compare":">","left":{"op":"sub","left":{"op":"sub","left":{"slot":"caller_record","when":"after"},"right":{"slot":"caller_record","when":"before"}},"right":{"op":"sub","left":{"slot":"contract_near","when":"after"},"right":{"slot":"contract_near","when":"before"}}},"right":{"number":"10000000000000000000000"}},{"compare":">","left":{"op":"sub","left":{"op":"sub","left":{"slot":"contract_near","when":"after"},"right":{"slot":"contract_near","when":"before"}},"right":{"op":"sub","left":{"slot":"caller_record","when":"after"},"right":{"slot":"caller_record","when":"before"}}},"right":{"number":"10000000000000000000000"}}]}
What each slot reads ($caller is the call's signer, $contract the contract, $arg:<name> that argument of the call;
any other name is an account your setup must create):
- caller_record: the caller's recorded vault balance. Reads {"view":{"method":"balance_of","args":{"account_id":"$caller"}}}
- contract_near: the contract account's real NEAR balance holding all deposits. Reads {"account":"$contract"}

Bug classes still open against R1 (attempts left in brackets):
1. state not restored when a cross-contract call or transfer fails [3]
2. state updated after an external call is scheduled, so a second call can slip in between [3]
3. a privileged method with no access check [3]
4. a callback that anyone can call directly [3]
5. attached deposits counted wrongly [3]
6. balance arithmetic that can overflow or underflow [3]
7. a callback starved of gas so it never runs [3]

For each open bug class, either give ONE Scenario that tries it, or skip it with a short reason why it cannot apply to this Rule and contract.
A skipped class is closed. On NEAR a transfer or cross-contract call a method schedules runs later, in its own receipt, after
the method's state changes are saved; if it fails (for example because the receiving account does not exist), only a callback
can undo those changes. So a method that changes state and schedules a transfer with no callback is a reason to TRY class 1,
not to skip it. A Scenario that does not break the Rule leaves its class open until its attempts run out.
Answer with JSON only:
{"attempts":[{"bugClass":<number>,"setup":[...],"call":{...}}],"skip":[{"bugClass":<number>,"reason":"<why>"}]}

Sandbox:
Before a Scenario starts the sandbox holds: the contract, deployed to account "vault", It was initialised by "owner" calling new({"owner":"owner"}).
and the accounts "owner" (100000000000000000000000000 yoctoNEAR). You can sign calls as any of these accounts and as any account your Scenario creates.
A Scenario is {"setup":[<action>,...],"call":<call>}:
- "setup" runs first, in order. Use it to create accounts, make deposits, and put the contract in the state you need.
- Then "before" is observed, then "call" (the ONE call under test) runs, then "after" is observed.
  The call must be to one of the Rule's methods. Gas refunds and returned deposits have landed before each observation.
Setup actions (all amounts are yoctoNEAR as decimal strings; 1 NEAR = 1000000000000000000000000; gas is a decimal string, at most 300000000000000):
- {"action":"createAccount","account":<short name such as "alice">,"balance":<yoctoNEAR>}  (the sandbox itself funds the new account)
- {"action":"call","as":<account>,"method":<contract method>,"args":{...},"deposit":<yoctoNEAR>,"gas":<gas>}
- {"action":"view","method":<contract view method>,"args":{...}}
The call under test: {"as":<account>,"method":<one of the Rule's methods>,"args":{...},"deposit":<yoctoNEAR>,"gas":<gas>}
Any account name may be one that does not exist (for example a receiver). A failed call does not stop the Scenario.
A call reserves its gas up front at about 0.1 NEAR per 100 Tgas (unused gas comes back), so a signer needs its deposit plus
that reserve: give each account you create at least 1 NEAR more than it will deposit, or attach less gas.

All Rules of this contract:
- R1: A deposit must change the caller's recorded balance by exactly the NEAR that really arrives on the contract account: never more (crediting a record with no funds behind it) and never less (taking funds without credit).
- R2: A withdraw must keep the caller's recorded loss and the receiver's real NEAR gain in step: either the transfer lands and the record loses exactly that amount, or the transfer fails and the record is put back whole; the receiver must never gain more or less than the record loses (a withdraw naming the contract itself as receiver is exempt, since that transfer lands back in the pot).
- R3: The withdrawal callback belongs to the contract alone: a call to on_withdraw_complete by anyone other than the contract itself must leave every recorded balance untouched, since no real NEAR moves on such a call.
- R4: Only the owner may pause or resume deposits; a call to set_deposits_paused by anyone else must leave the pause flag untouched.
- R5: Once the vault is deployed and initialized, calling new again must not replace the owner or reset the pause flag.

